scope prioritises the 1st ocurance of that symbol it finds on it's scope or it's ancestor scope

so if somewhere in a scope we have "a" that is a record
and somewhere else we have a "a" that is a int 
but if the latter is closer to the expresion 
we use that symbol in gets priority

as long as something is not on the local scope it is ok to define it

we need to first build a symbol table and be able to traverse it in the 2nd pass to do "lazy" eval
otherwise we will have the C/C++(in C++ the problem doens't exist inside classes for some reason) problem
and we will need to foward declare functions, types which is anoying
types will be ptr only if their declaration index is higher than the requesting token
functions are to do whatever they want
variables will be just like C/C++, I do not want a python system
some how I need to not have recursive relations...somehow

the concept of closures can be lowered to a language by having a the closure be a function
with a opaque pointer at the start as the state and then the argumetns
so we can group those together in one type that holds the @fn(state: @ptr; args...)(return type)
now for normal functions that we want to be passed to closure arguments we can implicitly convert them
by wrapping them in a function with the same arguments just with state: @ptr at the front

state can be renamed to ctx/ context I am not sure yet
maybe I should let the user to build the state by himself

interfaces and general pollymorphism can be achived by doing a interface @rec where we hold a @ptr and the "virtual" functions as function pointers
very simple and this is how Zig I think does it
allocator_factory_obj -> allocator_obj
this can add some overhead to our containers since if we want any other allocator than a global one (like clib_malloc) we need to pass it as a state to the record/tuple


consider lowering to C instead of LLVM-IR for simplicity
it will bite you in the long run and it will make generics a bit hellish, pefomance might be garbo but it will be easier to get going
LLVM is a very big dependency to have and does too many things
but also LLVM does many things
